 "prompt": "You are answering a biomedical research question.\n\nQuestion: Do inhaled corticosteroids affect perception of dyspnea during bronchoconstriction in asthma?\n\nAnswer \"yes\", \"no\", or \"maybe\", and give your confidence from 0 to 100 that your answer is correct.\n\nRespond with only a JSON object and no other text, in exactly this format:\n{\"answer\": \"yes\" or \"no\" or \"maybe\", \"confidence\": integer from 0 to 100}",
 "session_persisted": true,
 "text": "\n\n<｜DSML｜function_calls\n\n<｜DSML｜function_callsBased on my knowledge of asthma pathophysiology and pharmacology:\n\nInhaled corticosteroids (ICS) are anti-inflammatory medications that reduce airway inflammation in asthma. While they effectively prevent asthma exacerbations and reduce airway hyperresponsiveness, they do not have an immediate bronchodilating effect during acute bronchoconstriction.\n\nDuring bronchoconstriction (acute asthma attacks), the sensation of dyspnea (shortness of breath) arises from several factors:\n1. Mechanical loading of respiratory muscles\n2. Increased ventilatory drive \n3. Neural feedback from lung receptors\n\nICS work through their anti-inflammatory effects to prevent bronchoconstriction from occurring, but they do not acutely reverse bronchoconstriction once it has occurred. The perception of dyspnea during acute bronchoconstriction is more directly affected by bronchodilators (like beta-agonists) that can rapidly open airways.\n\nHowever, some research suggests that chronic ICS treatment may reduce airway hyperresponsiveness over time, which could indirectly affect dyspnea perception by reducing the frequency and severity of bronchoconstriction episodes. But during actual bronchoconstriction, ICS don't provide immediate relief.\n\n{\"answer\": \"no\", \"confidence\": 70}",
